<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ibn Tumart</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1130">1130</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Muhammad ibn Tumart</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ibn Tumart was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> religious reformer who preached a strict doctrine of the unity of God and proclaimed himself the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>. From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">Atlas Mountains</a> he led a movement against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire</a>, and died in 1130. His followers, led by Abd al-Mumin, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a> on his teachings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibn-tumart.jpg" alt="Ibn Tumart" width="269" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joan Serra y Pausas · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spanish_engraving_depicting_Ibn_Tumart.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Many details of Ibn Tumart's life were recorded by hagiographers, whose accounts probably blend in elements of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> doctrine. He was born between 1078 and 1082 in the village of Igiliz in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sous">Sous</a> region of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, a member of the Hargha, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> tribe of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-atlas">Anti-Atlas</a> belonging to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masmuda">Masmuda</a> confederation. His family was humble, and his father was a lamplighter at a mosque. He and his followers asserted that he descended from Idris I and so from al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-ibn-ali">Hasan ibn Ali</a>, a claim that modern scholars widely dispute. About 1106 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> to study under al-Turtushi, and then east, where he came under the influence of al-Ghazali's ideas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His main principle was a rigid unitarianism, tawhid, which denied that God has attributes as incompatible with his unity. He opposed the Almoravid sponsorship of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki school</a>, accusing it of neglecting the Sunnah and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a>, and objected to the public sale of wine and pork. His followers took the name al-Muwwahidun, those who affirm the unity of God, which Spanish authors rendered Almohades. He returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> in 1117/18, preaching a puritan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> from town to town, and about 1119 set up camp at Mellala, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bejaia">Bejaia</a>, where his first followers joined him, among them al-Bashir and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-mumin">Abd al-Mu'min</a>.</p><p>In 1120 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>, where he confronted the Almoravid emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-yusuf">Ali ibn Yusuf</a> and was expelled after a flogging of fourteen lashes. He returned to Igiliz and lived in a nearby cave. Towards the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramadan">Ramadan</a> in late 1121 he declared himself the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>, the expected divinely guided justicer, which was effectively a declaration of war on the Almoravid state. In 1122 or shortly after he founded a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribat">ribat</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tinmel">Tinmel</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">High Atlas</a> as the spiritual centre and military headquarters of the revolt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>For eight years the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> revolt was largely a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">Atlas</a> ravines and peaks. In early 1130 the Almohads besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> and were crushed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-al-buhayra">Battle of al-Buhayra</a>, with huge losses; al-Bashir was killed. Ibn Tumart died in August 1130. He had organised the Almohad commune as a pyramidal hierarchy of fourteen grades, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-ten">Council of Ten</a> and a wider Council of Fifty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masmuda">Masmuda</a> sheikhs. After a probable struggle for the succession, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-mumin">Abd al-Mu'min</a>, a Zenata Berber, was chosen, and led a seven-year campaign of conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> that culminated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-conquest-of-marrakesh">fall of Marrakesh</a> in 1147.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ibn Tumart” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its founder, <b>Muhammad ibn Tumart</b>, was a Berber theologian of the Masmuda who had studied in al-Andalus and the Islamic East.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Almohad movement, launched by <b>Ibn Tumart</b> in the Atlas Mountains around 1121, denounced the Almoravids as anthropomorphist heretics who had succumbed to luxury and oppressed their subjects with unlawful taxation, much the same charges the Almoravids had levelled against the taifas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hafsids took their name from Abu Hafs Umar al-Hintati, a Masmuda Berber chief from the High Atlas who was among the closest companions of <b>Ibn Tumart</b>, the founder of the Almohad movement, and a leading figure of the early Almohad Caliphate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Igiliz. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tinmel">Tinmel</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.99,-8.23&amp;year=1120" aria-label="Tinmel on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Ibn Tumart"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4399 4894h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4399 4894h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=30.99,-8.23&amp;year=1120">What was here in 1120? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
